Isolate the member template: data on one side, the managed core on the other
The reevaluation you asked for. `site-template/` is now the terse thing, and the
managed half is beside it.

```
site-template/          _data/site.yml, _data/programs.yml, .gitignore
member-site-core/       _config.yml, _layouts/, assets/css/, index.html, feed.xml, Gemfile
```

**A member's repository is two YAML files and a `.gitignore`.** No markup, no
config, no Gemfile. That is the answer to *"if we can get things done with just
a configuration."*

## First — your interpretation, and it is not what I did

> What I think you've done is make site template capable of being the basis of
> the canonical site too.

**No.** What I did was make the *builder* stop special-casing `site` — a site is
a directory with a `_config.yml`, so `site/` is one manifest entry like any
other. `site-template/` could never have initialized `site`: different layouts,
different data, different content, entirely different site. They are peers to
the builder, not interchangeable.

You said it does not necessarily need to be the case, and it isn't, so nothing
was built on that reading.

## The FCPM credit is gone, not made optional

> even seeing the option to show it gives me the ick. I see that on other sites,
> and it's like, it feels like we are petitioning them to, like, become a brand
> object, which isn't even false, but it feels greasy.

Three places, all removed:

- the footer block in the layout — deleted, not made conditional;
- `show_fcpm_credit` out of the member's `_data/site.yml`;
- and one you did not name: the managed config carried
  `description: A Fort Collins Public Media member site.` as a **default**. A
  default that says something about a member's site on their behalf is wrong on
  every site until somebody notices and corrects it. Title and description now
  come only from the member's own data.

**The composed output contains zero mentions of Fort Collins Public Media.**
Checked, not assumed.

## A collision is the eject signal

The builder stages `member-site-core/`, then the member's files on top. If a
member carries a file the core also provides, it reports **`diverged`** and
**refuses to build** rather than picking a winner.

Silently preferring ours would publish a site that is not the one they wrote,
which is the one unforgivable behaviour in a tenancy built on *we own nothing*.
It is the same fact `--ff-only` failing used to carry, noticed at build time
instead of at update time. For a tenant that is news, not a defect; for our own
scaffold it is a bug in the split, and the exit code says so.

This also settles the prune floor — **option 1, ejecting injects**. The template
prunes to data, and ejecting means composing the core into their repository as a
commit. Recorded as your call.

## The README, and the voice

`site-template/README.md` → `docs/member-sites.md`, because that one is for the
crew and *"would never land in a real site templated thing."*

**The template now has no README at all.** The audience-facing one is yours to
write, and it is deliberately absent rather than stubbed — a placeholder that
ships to every member site is precisely what somebody forgets to delete, which
is the argument the fixtures file already makes about invented example shows.

I also flagged in the moved file that its *"discoverability at a local level"*
framing is superseded by the burnout framing, rather than rewriting it in a
voice you have reserved.

## Your naming question has an answer already in the constellation

`member-site-core/` is a provisional name and says so in the tree. **The journal
engine is already exactly this thing** — I read it at
`station-node/.journal-engine`:

> the shared, **content-less Jekyll machinery** a civic node mounts at
> `.journal-engine/` … It ships layouts, includes, plugins, assets, `skel/`
> boilerplate, and `bin/` build scripts — **never articles.**

Your managed core is *the engine*. Your terse template is *`skel/`*. And the
term you thought it already had, for content with no index file, is **`journal/`
where disk path == URL path** — which is your wildcard-to-path-prefix idea
already named.

The sharpest borrowing is **`/NAME`**: one line, the address a build is meant to
be reached at, **site-owned and never engine-owned**, which `bin/sync.sh` is
forbidden from overwriting. Deliberately not `CNAME`, because *"canonical here
means a proven copy, not the only copy."* For a tenancy built on owning nothing,
that is governance written into a file format. TENANCY.md now carries the whole
comparison line by line.

**It also makes the folder-versus-submodule worry smaller than it looked.** Both
get submoduled, for different reasons — the engine because it is shared
machinery, each member site because it is somebody else's content we only
borrow. The cadence is weekly and the build is already sequential and isolated
per site, so hydrate a batch, build it, drop it. And committing the built tree
is what the five existing syncs already do at smaller size.

**I did not mount the journal engine.** Engine mounts are their own PR by
whoever owns it — STATION.md's rule since the node seat was seated — and this
PR is already the template reevaluation you asked for. Say the word and it is
the next one.

## Your build failure

`Error: Unknown engine site/.ruby on ubuntu-24.04` — `setup-ruby` was handed
`site/.ruby-version` as a path when it wants a literal. It was on the
`build-root-is-site` branch and **is already fixed** by commit `3889038`, before
that branch merged. Main is green; nothing is outstanding.

